## Section 4 — Domain Isolation (RLS) and Write Governance

Same enforcement pattern already locked for `expert_identity` in
TI-EP-002 §4 and TI-EP-003 §3.

```sql
ALTER TABLE commentary
ENABLE ROW LEVEL SECURITY;

ALTER TABLE commentary
FORCE ROW LEVEL SECURITY;

-- READ: same-domain OR active contract with READ/ADMIN scope
CREATE POLICY commentary_read_visibility
ON commentary
FOR SELECT
USING (
    domain_id = current_setting('app.current_domain', true)::uuid
    OR EXISTS (
        SELECT 1
        FROM domain_participation_contract dpc
        WHERE dpc.status = 'ACTIVE'
          AND dpc.access_scope IN ('READ','ADMIN')
          AND (
                (dpc.source_domain_id = current_setting('app.current_domain', true)::uuid
                 AND dpc.target_domain_id = commentary.domain_id)
             OR (dpc.target_domain_id = current_setting('app.current_domain', true)::uuid
                 AND dpc.source_domain_id = commentary.domain_id)
          )
    )
);

-- WRITE: same-domain only (no cross-domain write, matching
-- expert_identity's baseline)
CREATE POLICY commentary_write_own_domain
ON commentary
FOR INSERT
WITH CHECK (domain_id = current_setting('app.current_domain', true)::uuid);

CREATE POLICY commentary_update_own_domain
ON commentary
FOR UPDATE
USING (domain_id = current_setting('app.current_domain', true)::uuid)
WITH CHECK (domain_id = current_setting('app.current_domain', true)::uuid);

CREATE POLICY commentary_delete_own_domain
ON commentary
FOR DELETE
USING (domain_id = current_setting('app.current_domain', true)::uuid);
```

### 4.1 Expert Type Enforcement (resolved — see Section 5, item 2)

Postgres CHECK constraints cannot reference another table, so this
rule is enforced via trigger, not CHECK:

```sql
CREATE OR REPLACE FUNCTION enforce_commentary_source_type()
RETURNS TRIGGER AS $$
BEGIN
    IF NOT EXISTS (
        SELECT 1 FROM expert_identity
        WHERE expert_id = NEW.expert_id
          AND type = 'commentary_source'
    ) THEN
        RAISE EXCEPTION 'expert_id % is not type commentary_source', NEW.expert_id;
    END IF;
    RETURN NEW;
END;
$$ LANGUAGE plpgsql;

CREATE TRIGGER trg_commentary_expert_type_check
BEFORE INSERT OR UPDATE ON commentary
FOR EACH ROW
EXECUTE FUNCTION enforce_commentary_source_type();
```

### 4.2 Write Audit Logging (resolved — see Section 5, item 3)

`log_domain_write_attempt()` (originally defined in TI-EP-003 §3) is
updated here to use `TG_TABLE_NAME` instead of a hardcoded
`entity_type`, so it can be safely shared across tables:

```sql
CREATE OR REPLACE FUNCTION log_domain_write_attempt()
RETURNS TRIGGER AS $$
BEGIN
    INSERT INTO domain_cross_access_log (
        log_id, source_domain_id, target_domain_id,
        entity_type, entity_id, action, was_allowed, timestamp
    )
    VALUES (
        gen_random_uuid(),
        current_setting('app.current_domain', true)::uuid,
        COALESCE(NEW.domain_id, OLD.domain_id),
        TG_TABLE_NAME,
        COALESCE(NEW.commentary_id, NEW.expert_id, OLD.commentary_id, OLD.expert_id),
        TG_OP,
        true,
        now()
    );
    RETURN COALESCE(NEW, OLD);
END;
$$ LANGUAGE plpgsql;

CREATE TRIGGER trg_commentary_write_log
AFTER INSERT OR UPDATE OR DELETE ON commentary
FOR EACH ROW
EXECUTE FUNCTION log_domain_write_attempt();
```

**Known limitation, tracked for future correction:** the
`COALESCE(NEW.commentary_id, NEW.expert_id, ...)` pattern works for
the two tables currently sharing this trigger (`expert_identity`,
`commentary`) because their primary key columns are known and
distinct. Adding a third table with a different primary key name will
require either extending this COALESCE chain (fragile — a mismatch
fails silently, logging a NULL entity_id rather than raising an
error) or refactoring to pass the PK column name as a trigger
argument. Revisit before OSHA or CRM tables are attached to this same
trigger.

